# Socket install protection

This plugin adds a `beforeShellExecution` hook. Before Cursor runs a shell command, the hook looks for package installs (npm, npx, pnpm, yarn, pip, uv, uvx, and cargo). An install that does not run through Socket Firewall (`sfw`) is blocked. `sfw` checks packages that are downloaded from public registries against Socket, and blocks confirmed malware. It needs no Socket account and no API key. Packages that come from a local cache are not checked.

`npx` and `npm exec` are allowed when the binary is already in `node_modules/.bin`, or with `--no-install`. These are not checked: `uv run` without `--with`, `cargo build` and `cargo test` (which can download crates), and installs inside containers (`docker run`, `docker build`).

The hook runs with `node`. If `node` is not on the PATH that Cursor starts with, the hook cannot run and Cursor runs the command without the check.

## When a command is blocked

The block message starts with "Protected by Socket" and shows the same command with `sfw` in front.

1. If the message says sfw is not installed, run `npm install -g sfw` first. The hook allows that command.
2. Run the command from the message, for example `sfw npm install lodash`. Keep every flag and argument.
3. If the message says the command cannot be checked (for example, it is nested too deeply or encoded), write it again as a plain command with `sfw` in front of the package manager.
4. `sfw` does not support private or custom registries. If the project installs from one (for example, an `.npmrc` or `pip.conf` that points at a company registry), stop and ask the user what to do. Do not run the install another way.
5. Do not try to get around the check: do not change the command's spelling, put it in a script, or set environment variables to skip the check. If the user wants to turn the check off, tell them to set `SOCKET_INSTALL_GATE=off` in the environment Cursor starts with. A hook that runs with `--strict` ignores that setting.

If `sfw` itself reports a blocked package, stop and tell the user the package name and version. Do not install it another way. Offer `/socket-inspect` for a full review, or suggest a different package.

## Protect a project and its Cloud Agents

The plugin hook runs only where the plugin is installed. To protect everyone who opens the repository as a trusted workspace, including Cloud Agents, add a project hook. Cloud Agents run project hooks but not user hooks.

1. Copy `.cursor-plugin/hooks/socket-install-gate.mjs` from the Socket plugin into the repository as `.cursor/hooks/socket-install-gate.mjs`. You can also download it from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/SocketDev/skills/main/.cursor-plugin/hooks/socket-install-gate.mjs`. The file has no dependencies.
2. Add this to `.cursor/hooks.json`. Merge it with any hooks that are already there:

   ```json
   {
     "version": 1,
     "hooks": {
       "beforeShellExecution": [
         {
           "command": "node .cursor/hooks/socket-install-gate.mjs",
           "failClosed": true,
           "timeout": 10
         }
       ]
     }
   }
   ```

   With `failClosed: true`, Cursor blocks the command when the hook cannot run, for example when it times out. This also blocks every shell command on a computer without `node`, so use it only where everyone has Node.js.

3. For Cloud Agents, also check the packages that the environment installs before the agent starts. In `.cursor/environment.json`, install `sfw` and put it in front of the install command:

   ```json
   {
     "install": "npm install -g sfw && sfw npm ci"
   }
   ```

   Use the repository's own install command (`sfw pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, `sfw pip install -r requirements.txt`, and so on). Keep the rest of `environment.json` as it is.

   Cursor uses this file only when the Cloud Agents environment is set to read `environment.json` from the repository. If the environment was set up in the Cursor dashboard, put the same `sfw` install command in its install script there. Self-hosted workers need it in their own setup.

## Protect a whole company

Cursor loads enterprise hooks from a fixed path on each computer. A device management (MDM) tool puts the files there:

| OS          | hooks.json                                       |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| macOS       | `/Library/Application Support/Cursor/hooks.json` |
| Linux / WSL | `/etc/cursor/hooks.json`                         |
| Windows     | `C:\ProgramData\Cursor\hooks.json`               |

Put `socket-install-gate.mjs` in the same folder, and use the hooks.json from the project section with that path in `command`. Add `--strict` so that `SOCKET_INSTALL_GATE=off`, which a `sessionStart` hook can also set, does not turn the check off. For example, on macOS: `node "/Library/Application Support/Cursor/socket-install-gate.mjs" --strict`. Install Node.js on every computer before you set `failClosed: true`.

By default, Cursor waits only a short time for hooks to load, so the first prompt after a restart can run before the hook is ready. On Enterprise plans, turn on **Block until hooks are ready** in the dashboard's hook settings.

Hooks added in the Cursor team dashboard cannot set `failClosed`, so a crash in the hook lets the command run. Use MDM or a project hook when the check must not fail open.

Admins who want one policy across agents, repositories, and CI can connect a Socket account at https://socket.dev/.
